PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The core model is competitive screening (Rothschild-Stiglitz style) by multiple principals (homeowners) offering menus to agents (handymen) with private types. Utilities imply c_H(v)=v, c_L(v)=v². θ parameters set type-specific outside options/reservations (not normalized to 0), with θ_L(v) depending on chosen v, and feasibility v≤θ. Pr(H)=5/6 is high. Key constraints: IR_H (p_H-v_H≥30), IR_L (p_L-v_L²≥θ_L(v)), IC_H/IC_L, plus zero-profit competition (p≈v net of outside options) on each contract taken. 

Major split: ~half of colleagues conclude no separating eq. exists (IC/IR cannot hold simultaneously with zero profits/competition; high Pr(H) invites pooling deviations; H-IR contradicts p≤v). Others propose specific pairs: ((20,400),(30,900)) (IR_L binds at p=v², 10-unit θ gap deters mimicry) or variants like ((15,225),(30,240)) (corner at θ_L max) or first-best with p=v. 

Disagreements center on whether θ enter as binding reservations (making large p necessary) vs. irrelevant, and whether competition forces unraveling. Errors noted: some ignore θ(v) dependence or assume zero reservations. Current belief: no separating eq. survives all constraints+competition; most consistent view is non-existence. Unresolved: exact mapping of θ to payoffs.}

